# The revision of libviprs/acadsharp-rs that this suite is tested against.
#
# `Cargo.toml` depends on that crate as `{ path = "../acadsharp-rs" }`, so CI has
# to lay a checkout down beside this one, and this file says which commit it
# gets. `.github/actions/clone-counterpart` fetches exactly this sha, as a sha,
# and then checks that the checkout really landed on it. There is no branch
# name, no repository variable and no fallback: `libviprs-org` resolved an unset
# repository variable to the empty string, which `actions/checkout` reads as
# "the default branch", and its gate then compared against whatever moved that
# morning, and `libviprs`' integration job clones a same-named counterpart
# branch, so a stale branch that shares a name builds against the wrong tree
# (libviprs#1013).
#
# The merged-only rule: this may only name a commit that is on `acadsharp-rs`'
# `main`. The action checks it through the compare API and accepts `identical`
# or `behind`, because a pin naming an older commit that is still an ancestor of
# `main` is merged. `ahead` or `diverged` is refused. A pin has to name
# something that will still be there tomorrow, and a PR head can be force-pushed
# or closed.
#
# The first line below that is neither blank nor a comment is the pin, it is the
# whole line, and it is 40 lowercase hex characters. A sha written inside one of
# these comments is prose: `tools/counterpart.sh rev` reads the payload line,
# not the first 40-hex run in the file, which is what `libviprs-tests` does and
# is a real way to lose a pin inside a bump note.
#
# To bump it: put an `acadsharp-rs` `main` commit here, run the suite against a
# sibling checkout at that commit, and say below what moved.
